# Expectation record for 05_pointers/003_struct_member_access.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 003_struct_member_access
area               = 05_pointers
description        = Member access through pointers including nested members, array members and a self-referential list
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
Every member accessed exists in the struct being accessed, and every array member is
indexed inside its declared bounds: vec has 4 elements indexed 0..3, arr has 3 elements
indexed 0..2, and table has 3 elements indexed 0..2. The linked list n1 -> n2 -> n3 is
statically initialized with a null tail, is finite and acyclic, and the walk stops when
the next pointer is null, so no null pointer is ever dereferenced. Writes go only to
objects that are not const.
No signed integer overflow, no shift out of range, no aliasing violation, no read of
uninitialized storage, no object modified twice between sequence points, at most one
side-effecting argument per call, and no dependence on padding bytes or on the relative
addresses of unrelated objects. The only external symbol is printf; no header is included,
no file, socket, environment variable, clock or random source is touched, and every input
is a literal in this source file.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
No member offset and no struct size is printed, so struct layout differences could not
affect the output even if they existed; only member VALUES and element-count differences
are printed.
No address or pointer value is printed: pointer facts reach the output only as equality
comparison results and as element-count differences, and every one of those differences is
cast to long long before printing, so a 32-bit ptrdiff_t on i686 and a 64-bit ptrdiff_t
elsewhere render identically. Plain char is never printed in a signedness-dependent way.
The only conversion specifiers this program uses are %d and %lld, both of which are
target-width independent.
END
